WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.942 --> 00:00:04.340
In this shot from 1950's
"Destination Moon,"

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.620
these stars were actually
2,000 light bulbs

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.420
from car headlights.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.700
It was probably the most
realistic portrayal of space

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:13.980
at the time,

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:18.237
but 68 years and several
real-life space missions later,

00:00:18.237 --> 00:00:21.468
"First Man" used
state-of-the-art LED screens

00:00:21.468 --> 00:00:23.126
to project realistic renders

00:00:23.126 --> 00:00:26.120
of the solar system
and the moon's surface,

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:29.740
even adding a reflection
on Ryan Gosling's helmet.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.020
It took decades of technical breakthroughs

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:33.640
to get to this point,

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.550
from intricate wirework
and robotic cameras

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:40.040
to CG legs floating in zero gravity.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:45.040
Here's how space scenes have
transformed over 120 years.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:48.460
Long before humankind took
their first steps on the moon,

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:51.920
filmmakers sought to bring
their stories to space.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.186
In 1902's "A Trip to the Moon,"

00:00:54.186 --> 00:00:56.827
director Georges Méliès
created the illusion

00:00:56.827 --> 00:00:58.680
of a capsule landing in the moon

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.910
using a substitution splice,

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:03.210
first shooting the actor playing the moon,

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.460
then adding the capsule in another shot

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.760
and combining the two frames.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.092
While this iconic shot
is obviously fantastical,

00:01:11.092 --> 00:01:12.708
"Destination Moon" wanted to be

00:01:12.708 --> 00:01:15.580
as scientifically accurate as possible.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:16.830
However, the filmmakers

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:20.262
had both a limited scientific
understanding of space

00:01:20.262 --> 00:01:23.540
and a limited toolbox for special effects.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:25.190
In addition to using light bulbs

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:27.120
for the stars in deep space,

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.360
the cast also used a matte
painting as a backdrop

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.542
with small cracks to create the illusion

00:01:32.542 --> 00:01:34.440
of perspective on the moon.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.760
But the biggest innovation at the time?

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:38.050
Wires.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:39.907
To create the illusion of low gravity,

00:01:39.907 --> 00:01:42.380
director Irving Pichel attached the actors

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:44.860
to piano wire run through a pulley system

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.493
and controlled by an overhead crane.

00:01:47.493 --> 00:01:50.270
This effectively lifted
the actors off the ground,

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.910
but keeping the wires
from showing up on camera

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:54.430
was a lot of work.

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:57.030
According to writer Robert
Heinlein, the set lights

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:00.270
could make the wires visible
on camera during filming.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.120
If the wires were seen in the take,

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.850
they'd have to start all over again.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.360
The movie also wanted to
show characters moving

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.880
as the ship left Earth's orbit.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.450
To create the illusion of a person

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.570
walking around all sides of the ship,

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:14.686
the crew built the set

00:02:14.686 --> 00:02:17.111
with a fixed camera and two gimbals.

00:02:17.111 --> 00:02:20.320
That way, the camera could
rotate in sync with the set.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.390
It was similar to the rig
that allowed Fred Astaire

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:26.530
to famously dance on walls
in 1951's "Royal Wedding."

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:29.810
All of these innovations
took a lot of time and money.

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:33.995
As Heinlein wrote, "Realism
is confoundedly expensive."

00:02:33.995 --> 00:02:35.100
Perhaps for that reason,

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.347
space scenes throughout
the 1950s and 1960s

00:02:38.347 --> 00:02:40.013
frequently relied on the audience's

00:02:40.013 --> 00:02:41.921
suspension of disbelief.

00:02:41.921 --> 00:02:43.540
Take this shot of a flying saucer

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.250
in "Plan 9 from Outer Space."

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:48.080
It's clearly just a toy on a string,

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.630
but that would all change
drastically in 1968

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:54.710
with "2001: A Space Odyssey."

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.310
Now that humans had
actually gone to space,

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.483
Stanley Kubrick needed to redefine

00:02:59.483 --> 00:03:02.750
what futuristic life in
space would look like.

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:06.400
And he did so by pushing the
limits of visual effects.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.330
When it came to wires, Kubrick found

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:11.047
that by simply placing
the camera on the ground,

00:03:11.047 --> 00:03:12.210
the actors' bodies

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:15.080
would naturally block the wires from view.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.370
But the zero-g illusions
got much more elaborate.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:21.160
The team built a 38-foot-tall centrifuge

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.600
that rotated at 3 miles per hour

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:25.563
to imitate artificial gravity.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.990
Unlike the rotating set
in "Destination Moon,"

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:30.680
this one had two cameras:

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.730
a mounted one that
moved along with the set

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:35.830
and a dolly camera that
moved with the actors.

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:37.950
But not all the sets were gigantic.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.690
These models were much
more detailed, elaborate,

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:43.730
and closer to real NASA
spacecraft at the time,

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:47.660
thanks to Kubrick's famously
obsessive attention to detail.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.740
To make the miniatures
look like they were moving,

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.460
the camera was driven along the track

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:53.950
on a motorized mount around the models.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:57.800
This method made it possible
to exactly repeat camera moves.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.830
The crew would do one
pass of the Discovery,

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:01.290
another pass of the stars,

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:04.520
and then combine it all
into one seamless shot.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:05.785
Kubrick basically created

00:04:05.785 --> 00:04:08.780
an early form of motion-control cameras.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:11.803
And these new cameras
revolutionized the space saga

00:04:11.803 --> 00:04:13.490
with "Star Wars."

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:17.080
With "A New Hope," George
Lucas wanted to create

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:20.600
awe-inspiring space chases and battles,

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:23.090
and audiences could be right in the action

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:25.740
thanks in part to a man named John Dykstra

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:29.410
and his new camera,
called the Dykstraflex.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:31.680
The Dykstraflex was
programmed by a computer

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.840
to move around the
miniatures in such a way

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.600
to create the illusion of
speeding through space,

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:40.020
like this imposing angle of the underbelly

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:41.510
of a Star Destroyer

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:43.840
or this shot during
the Death Star dogfight

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.500
that appears to be from
a pilot's point of view.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:48.150
The camera's precise programming

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:52.260
also made shots easily repeatable
for seamless compositing.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:54.700
It's why multiple models
could be shot separately

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:57.000
and then combined during the dogfight.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.250
It's also why the Death Star

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:00.620
really looks like it's
floating up in space,

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:03.840
when in reality this close-up
was shot in a parking lot.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:05.640
Motion-control cameras can be seen

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:07.770
again and again in space movies.

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:09.500
They were used in the "Star Wars" sequels

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:12.773
throughout the 1980s and
all the way through 2014

00:05:12.773 --> 00:05:15.270
for the practical models
of "Interstellar."

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:19.120
But all of these movies were
about fictional space travel.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:23.270
Things needed to be a lot more
realistic with "Apollo 13."

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:24.720
Houston, we have a problem.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:25.840
Oh, boy, what's going on here?

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:27.070
Flight, let me get back to you.

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:29.110
Narrator: So director
Ron Howard put the cast

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:31.980
in actual zero-g for wider shots.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:36.010
It was the first movie
ever shot on NASA's KC-135,

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:38.510
where real astronauts
trained for space conditions

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:39.430
at the time.

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:42.420
The plane flies up 35,000 feet in the air

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:44.680
before plunging back to Earth.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:48.750
This renders all passengers
weightless for 25 seconds --

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.170
no wires needed.

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:53.050
The crew only had 25
seconds per ride to shoot,

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:56.480
so the filmmakers broke down
each scene into small chunks

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:59.760
and then later used wires
on set for close-ups.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:03.310
In total, the cast and
crew took 612 flights,

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:06.760
adding up to nearly four
hours of weightlessness.

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:09.040
Shooting on the Vomit
Comet looks authentic,

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:10.660
but the limited zero-g windows

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:12.770
make it a logistical nightmare.

00:06:12.770 --> 00:06:16.473
And what do you do for shots
that need to be much longer?

00:06:18.027 --> 00:06:21.130
"Gravity" contained harrowing
continuous takes in space

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:23.900
that lasted as long as 15 minutes,

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:25.662
making it difficult to both move

00:06:25.662 --> 00:06:27.511
and light the actors properly.

00:06:27.511 --> 00:06:30.860
We went to incredible lengths
on the movie "Gravity"

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:34.360
to portray microgravity or zero gravity.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:35.570
Narrator: First, the crew relied

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:37.500
on previsualized animations

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.220
that showed what the movie would look like

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:40.910
before shooting took place.

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:42.675
Studying hours of reference footage

00:06:42.675 --> 00:06:44.030
to create the digital characters

00:06:44.030 --> 00:06:47.198
also helped them imitate
weightlessness more accurately.

00:06:47.198 --> 00:06:48.610
Chris: People do not move slowly

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:50.460
as though they're underwater in space.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:52.150
That's a common misconception.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:53.190
If you watch those astronauts,

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:54.765
they move really quickly,

00:06:54.765 --> 00:06:56.515
'cause there's no air resistance.

00:06:56.515 --> 00:06:57.500
Narrator: To tackle the sequence

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:59.490
where a debris storm hits the characters,

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:01.148
director Alfonso Cuarón

00:07:01.148 --> 00:07:03.414
and cinematographer Emmanuel Lubezki

00:07:03.414 --> 00:07:06.505
invented a new technology
called a light box.

00:07:06.505 --> 00:07:08.613
Chris: We were going to run for 15 minutes

00:07:08.613 --> 00:07:10.000
without a hard cut.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.530
We had to be able to plan the lighting

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:12.900
and have perfect continuity,

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.410
because everything was going
to need to sit together.

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:18.310
Narrator: The light box
built on existing technology.

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:19.820
Sandra Bullock and George Clooney

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:22.500
were strapped into tilt rigs
while their space journey

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:25.330
was captured by motion-control cameras.

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:28.750
All of that was inside a
box covered with LED panels

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:30.020
that could, for example,

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:33.850
show real images of Earth
from space as the backdrop.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:35.480
Because it was all programmed,

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:36.670
they could change up the lighting

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:38.870
faster than any normal studio light,

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:40.490
which came in handy for the scene

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:42.790
where they're hit by space debris.

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:45.500
Movies like "First Man"
built on the light box

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:48.800
and went even further with LED screens.

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:50.250
Director Damien Chazelle

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:54.530
used a 35-foot-tall,
65-foot-wide LED screen

00:07:54.530 --> 00:07:59.240
to provide dynamic 180-degree
views of space in real time.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:01.950
That meant the Gemini
space capsule leaving Earth

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:03.860
could be captured in-camera.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:06.380
And because this life-size
model was motorized,

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:08.930
the camera didn't need
to fake any movements.

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:11.930
You could even get this
convincing lunar reflection

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:13.680
on Ryan Gosling's helmet.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:16.810
Making the moon realistic
took a lot of effort as well.

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:20.090
Neil Armstrong's moonwalk
was shot in a quarry at night

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:21.690
that was manicured with gravel

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:23.920
to replicate the moon's surface.

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:25.560
Later, that footage was combined

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:29.310
with real surface scans of
the moon provided by NASA.

00:08:29.310 --> 00:08:30.930
It's the tiny details like this

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:32.900
that really sold "First Man."

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:33.900
Look at this shot.

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:36.220
The extra dust that clouds each footstep

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:39.040
was added entirely with CG.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:40.810
And CGI might be the answer

00:08:40.810 --> 00:08:43.940
when it comes to truly
mimicking zero-g movement.

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:45.440
Gravity's impossible to get away from.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:46.920
You just, you're constantly under it.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:48.770
Narrator: Framestore made great strides

00:08:48.770 --> 00:08:50.910
in digital doubles in "The Midnight Sky,"

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:54.170
making weightlessness look
more real than ever before.

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:55.910
You hold your limb out and swing around,

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:58.580
you kind of feel gravity doing its thing.

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:00.540
We were able to completely eliminate that.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:02.230
Narrator: Digital doubles
allowed filmmakers

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:04.660
to conquer more challenging
weightless scenes,

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:07.930
like these wide shots of
crew outside the Aether.

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:10.140
This actor was minimally wired up on set,

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:12.670
but his legs were swapped
out with a digital pair

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:15.700
so there was no hint
of real-world gravity.

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:17.950
Fewer wires meant they
didn't have to worry about

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:19.249
where the camera was placed,

00:09:19.249 --> 00:09:21.370
as they did in "Space Odyssey."

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:24.020
The team also used a virtual camera system

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:26.180
where the cinematographer
referenced an iPad

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:28.280
showing rough animated footage.

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:29.960
By pointing it around an empty room,

00:09:29.960 --> 00:09:32.320
he could physically block out the shots.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:34.560
This level of planning
allowed the spacewalk

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:38.310
to feature 70 fully CG performances.

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:40.360
Chris: The technology has
come so far in the 10 years

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:41.200
since we did "Gravity"

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:43.480
that you can do these things
in quite a lightweight way,

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:45.550
and it is interactive and
easy enough for a director

00:09:45.550 --> 00:09:47.640
to just hold an iPad
and look around the set.

00:09:47.640 --> 00:09:49.300
Narrator: The result is a series of shots

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:51.160
where digital movements, wire stunts,

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:54.460
and even hidden crew members
lifting objects into the air

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:56.340
all contribute to make space travel

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:59.110
both terrifying and awe-inspiring.

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:01.650
Chris: So we've come a
long way since "Apollo 13,"

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:04.420
and, indeed, we've come a
long way since "Gravity."

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:06.360
Narrator: Méliès made a capsule fly

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:09.330
so Kubrick could defy the laws of gravity

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:13.460
so Cuarón could capture the
light of the solar system.

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:16.530
Each might seem like a
small step on its own,

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:20.550
but taken all together, it
was one giant leap for cinema,

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:22.756
one dust cloud at a time.

